# Test fixture 15 — the back-projection format.
#
# Per spec §4.2: "When settlement runs (Fate-driven liquid-type
# inference + kintsugi), it appends three things below `---`:
# (1) the settlement header (date + Contract OID + settlement chain);
# (2) custom property definitions [no change here unless settlement
# refined them]; (3) implementation blocks per declared property,
# one per (property, glass) pair."
#
# This fixture shows what a SETTLED file looks like — a contract
# above `---`, then the canonical back-projection layout below. The
# `\` holes inside `implementation` are Fate-filled at settlement;
# this fixture leaves them as `\` (the seed shape).
#
# Expected: parse OK (above `---`); below-`---` parses as a settled
#           region with a header, an `implementation` block, and a
#           `\` hole inside the body. The Contract OID matches the
#           hash of the above-`---` region (the staleness check, per
#           spec §4.3).

in @code/rust
in @epistemologic/property/halts

grammar @test/15/back_projection {
  glass to @code/rust {
    property halts
  }
}

---

# Back-projected after settlement on 2026-06-15.
# Contract OID: 4a3f8b2c9d1e6f5a8b3c2d1e6f5a8b3c2d1e6f5a8b3c2d1e6f5a8b3c2d1e6f5a
# Settlement: kintsugi(fate(liquid_inference(@code/rust AST)))
# These implementations are mechanical artifacts. Re-settle to regenerate.

implementation halts for @code/rust glass {
  # Walks Rust AST: rejects unbounded `loop`, recursive calls without
  # decreasing measure, async/await without timeout. Fate selects the
  # concrete AST-walker body during settlement; the `\` is the hole.
  \
}
